    "content" : "(b) For positive β values."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 69,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 496.667, 605.223, 507.333, 614.557 ],
    "font" : null,
    "font size" : 12.0,
    "content" : "12"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 74,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 69.333, 493.557, 525.0, 566.557 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1000",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Figure 6: T 22 vs. r ( T 22 unit: 10 4 Pa). In the first panel, at r = 0 (the crack-tip), negative β does not reduce T 22 compared with T 22 at β = 0 . Generally, a more negative value does not necessarily generate greater T 22 at the crack tip. In the second panel for β > 0 , positive β generates smaller T 22 value compared with T 22 at β = 0 . Greater β creates smaller T 22 at r = 0 . Slightly wavy patterns also appear in all the curves due to the effect of the density-dependent moduli."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H1",
    "id" : 75,
    "level" : "Doctitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 71.0, 458.223, 133.0, 472.89 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "SFBX1440",
    "font size" : 14.346,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "5.3 SIF"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 76,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.333, 394.89, 524.667, 450.223 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1095",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "For the current problem, we do not have an asymptotic or analytical solution. Therefore, an explicit description of crack-tip SIF is not available. However, one can utilize the crack-tip SIF defined for the linear elastic fracture mechanics model and use the finite element solution for the nonlinear model to glean some vital physical insight into SIF."
  }, {
    "type" : "formula",
    "pdfua_tag" : "Formula",
    "id" : 77,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 248.333, 337.89, 524.333, 356.89 ],
    "content" : "KI lim 2TrT22. (33) r-0+"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 78,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 70.333, 164.223, 525.667, 328.223 ],
    "font" : "SFRM1095",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "It is challenging to analytically compute K I for complicated models such as the ones in 3-D that we are investigating in this paper. We compute K I numerically for illustrative purposes to understand its value as a function of the nonlinear modeling parameter β . The function √ 2 πrT 22 vs. r is shown in Fig. 7. For β = 0 , − 10 , − 20 , − 30 , the K I values are, respectively, 0.0110, 0.0185, 0.0251, 0.0178; For β = 0 , 10 , 20 , 30 , the K I values are, respectively, 0.0110, 0.0060, 0.0037, 0.0026 (Unit: 10 4 mm 1 / 2 Pa). All the K I values are very close, implying an identical theoretical K I value for all β is highly likely. Such numerical approximation for K I shows that the nonlinear model with density-dependent moduli is designed appropriately under the common crack criteria used for the linear-elasticity fracture model. Also, in the two panels of Fig. 7, the curve shapes for β < 0 and β > 0 are slightly distinct. The β < 0 curves show a greater curvature, while those for β > 0 show flatter behaviors. Such difference is caused by the sign of β , disclosing how negative and positive β values impact the SIF differently."
  } ]
